== interview 18-30,55-60,95-125
    18	
    19	## What makes the design MPC rather than a threshold rule?
    20	
    21	It evaluates future action sequences against a prediction model and constrained
    22	objective, executes the first action and reobserves before replanning.
    23	CPU-tested planner components exist; E2 accepts exact candidate ordering within
    24	its full-action Fixed-reference scope, not runtime integration or native MPC.
    25	A future complete-loop claim needs real application and reobservation receipts.
    26	The current nominal continuation adds the E6 Fixed-timing correction and E8
    27	Train-only scenario generation; those are component results, not native-loop
    28	qualification.
    29	
    30	## What is the objective?
    55	
    56	## What is the difference from Fixed, FIFO and OneMove?
    57	
    58	Fixed holds a development-selected budget. FIFO specifies service order and
    59	needs an explicit admission/resource policy; otherwise it may duplicate Fixed.
    60	Strong OneMove searches a best allowed single transition with the same model
    95	## What do the existing measurements establish?
    96	
    97	E1 provides audited historical native serving measurements under scheduled
    98	budgets; E2/E3 provide bounded CPU component evidence. E4 is a partial native
    99	observation diagnostic whose component coverage failed. None establishes a fair
   100	native MPC win. A/B were on different hosts and are not a causal comparison.
   101	Each offered 7,643 requests in 300 seconds: A rejected 5,667, B rejected 5,584;
   102	572 and 736, respectively, met the TTFT SLO. Those deliberately varied,
   103	scheduled-budget overload captures are identification data, not controller
   104	performance.
   105	
   106	## What if MPC loses?
   107	
   108	Publish the actual table and scope, then explain model error, overhead, control
   109	authority or workload sensitivity only where evidence supports the explanation.
   110	The accounting, instrumentation, bounded runtime components and audit trail
   111	remain engineering contributions; portable clean-checkout reproduction is
   112	still a P0 item. A negative result is better than an inflated claim.
   113	
   114	## Can this go on a resume before native MPC succeeds?
   115	
   116	Yes, using deliverable A's [scoped bullets](resume_bullets.md): implemented
   117	gateway/accounting, instrumentation, replay and CPU-tested planning, with their
   118	evidence boundaries. Do not claim a native MPC deployment, measured speedup or
   119	complete clean-checkout benchmark reproduction.
   120	
   121	Deliverable B separately requires two consecutive timestamped real control
   122	cycles, actual admission-behavior evidence, a Train-frozen strong Fixed
   123	baseline, held-out comparison, reproduction and independent audit.
   124	Superiority, production deployment and a universal SLO theorem are not
   125	prerequisites. B's incompleteness does not block A or applications.
